      "writtenOn": "2023-07-07T15:34:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Typically, the size of the seed should be at least as large as the size of the random array that the PRNG is supposed to generate.\n\nWait, this confuses me:\n\n1. Doesn\u0027t a PRNG work by seeding it once with a seed (order of magnitude: tens of bytes) before using it to produce _any_ amount of random bytes (potentially Gigs of data)?\n1. If the PRNG requires as many bytes of seed as it produces of pseudo-entropy then what\u0027s the point of the PRNG? Cryptographically, why not use the entropy contained in the seed in the first place? And, in our particular case of `vmbase`, what are we gaining from adding support for a PRNG if it doesn\u0027t reduce the pressure on the TRNG?\n\n\u003e [1] https://www.openssl.org/docs/man1.0.2/man3/RAND_bytes.html\n\nDo we want to expose both `RAND_bytes` and `RAND_pseudo_bytes` to clients? Are those actually mapping to TRNG and PRNG, respectively? If we keep the seed secret from the host, isn\u0027t `RAND_pseudo_bytes` enough for generating the DICE salt stored in the instance.img?",
      "parentUuid": "27e6e86c_67e90260",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 72,
        "startChar": 37,
        "endLine": 72,
        "endChar": 39
      },
      "revId": "d5879d7e72f397ad8a06d4556beeeea440c96233",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
