      "writtenOn": "2023-07-07T15:34:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "That\u0027s a sound architecture and I like the idea of completely wrapping away TRNG (in the end, can\u0027t we assume the TRNG to only ever be dedicated to seeding the PRNG?). What do you think of keeping 2 client-facing modules: `vmbase::rand` (or `::prng`, although a more generic name is probably better) for simply generating random bytes and `crypto` for actual crypto operations such as AEAD?\n\nI find it confusing that a client only interested in random values would need to import (and initialize?) `vmbase::crypto`. Both modules could internally be built on top of a `bssl` module (either coming from `vmbase` or from `libs/`) where most of the code currently existing in `pvmfw/crypto.rs` (including the error handling stuff) would go. Alternatively, the TRNG and PRNG could be initialized on the first access, like `get_hypervisor`(?)\n\nRegarding function names, I agree that having the name `prng::seed()` is much clearer than `prng::init()` but there\u0027s more to initializing the PRNG than seeding it as the TRNG needs to be initialized (and, if we support more than one, selected) so `rand::init` would abstract away those considerations in a way that\u0027s clear enough to the blissfully oblivious client. Of course, internally, the PRNG-seeding function should be named `seed`!",
      "parentUuid": "affffbc6_777ad134",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 169,
        "startChar": 7,
        "endLine": 169,
        "endChar": 16
      },
